{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1101464-where-is-the-musical-motif-in-2ed","id":1101464,"title":"Where is the musical motif in 2ed?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-06-07T19:26:38-07:00","id":1101464,"title":"Where is the musical motif in 2ed?","content":"The book was excellent but I don't recall that there's any mention for why vampires refer to their immortality as &quot;the Requiem&quot;, which makes the title kinda pointless.<br /> <br /> sure, the vampire's true nature is referred to as &quot;Dirge&quot; but that's pretty much as far as it goes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12561-shawarbaaz","name":"Shawarbaaz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12561_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"467"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-07T20:07:51-07:00","id":1101476,"title":"","content":"A Requiem is also &quot;a mass for the repose of the souls of the dead,&quot; so I think that's where the name comes from.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://eoinsstuff.shoutwiki.com/wiki/Genius:_the_Transgression_(2nd_Edition)\" target=\"_blank\">Genius: the Transgression 2E is a thing that's being worked on.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4488-malonkey1","name":"malonkey1","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4488_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-07T22:36:28-07:00","id":1101504,"title":"","content":"Its right there on the fist non-table-of-contents page.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What are you going to do to make it through tonight? What about tomorrow night? And after the deeds are done and your belly\u2019s full, how are you going to live with yourself? What are you going to do with your damnation that makes it worth all the sins along the way?<br /> That\u2019s the Requiem.<br /> That\u2019s only half the question, though. Mortals are dinner, but they\u2019re also what you\u2019ve got for dates. No matter how<br /> callous you become, you\u2019ll need to move among them. How will you keep your connection to Humanity, even as a sham?<br /> That\u2019s the Masquerade.<br /> The song and the dance don\u2019t always play well. Devote yourself to redeeming human sinners and you may discover they\u2019re the only creatures you understand.<br /> Spend your nights in a vault perfecting your monstrosity and you may find yourself trapped, unable to flee through the masses when the hunters bash down the gates. </div> </div> </div>The book is also full to the brim with vampires' night-to-night lives being referred to as their &quot;Requiem(s)&quot; so I'm not sure what you're missing. A simple ctrl + F of the PDF reveals a plethora of such uses.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-07T23:56:03-07:00","id":1101512,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1101464-where-is-the-musical-motif-in-2ed?p=1101504#post1101504\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Its right there on the fist non-table-of-contents page.<br /> <br /> The book is also full to the brim with vampires' night-to-night lives being referred to as their &quot;Requiem(s)&quot; so I'm not sure what you're missing. A simple ctrl + F of the PDF reveals a plethora of such uses.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well 1ed started with a metaphor right at the opening fiction, it also recommends actual Requiems by Mozart and Dvorak, and some vampires used it as a figure of speech &quot;I mean in the notes and chords of the Requiem. I\u2019m nearly 40 years dead.&quot;<br /> <br /> This by no means a criticism, I just felt an element was missing but it doesn't matter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12561-shawarbaaz","name":"Shawarbaaz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12561_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"467"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-08T00:10:59-07:00","id":1101516,"title":"","content":"Maybe because Vampire: The Dead Man's Party would have led to a lawsuit. Vampires view their existence as a dance for the poetry, as in they move around without really going anywhere in their &quot;lives&quot;. Music is associated with dancing. Someone thought it was cute to speak in terms related to practices associated with the dead before the disposal of the corpse for the walking corpses.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-08T13:30:13-07:00","id":1101715,"title":"","content":"Strictly speaking, a Requiem was not originally the music which accompanied a funeral mass, but rather the term for the mass for the dead itself. Requiem is a bit of synecdoche from the first line of the introit. The introit for this mass begins, in Latin, &quot;Requiem, aeternum dona eis, Domine...&quot;, which translates as &quot;Grant them eternal rest, O Lord.&quot; The word requiem is, somewhat oddly, in the singular accusative. That is, the introit is directing the Lord to &quot;rest them&quot;. English doesn't really have a comparable construction. The closest might be the much more clinical &quot;sedate them&quot;.<br /> <br /> This could be read, for purposes of the game, in several different ways.<ul><li>Maybe, we as players hope to create a character that can eventually reach a place of peace and repose.</li> <li>In game, the world tends to be chaotic and dangerous, rest would be a hoped for, but rarely achieved, luxury.</li> <li>Perhaps, the characters are demanding that God grant them ease and rest when they finally meet the final death.</li> <li>OTOH, it may have more to do with all the people vampires tend to kill. Maybe they are in need of rest, and the mercy of God.</li> <li>Maybe, it implies that while exciting and alluring these are, after all, monsters. The world would be better off if they were fully dead.</li> <li>A hunter, in combat with a vampire, may call upon God to grant these creatures their final repose, with some help from the hunter, if necessary.</li> </ul><br /> It's only in the last four or five centuries, recent in the history of Christendom, that there were popular performances of the music which was commonly used in the mass for the dead. Picture it. A music hall in 1750 and someone says, &quot;You know what would be fun? Let's get kind of drunk and play the music we play when somebody dies. It's going to be played for all of us sooner or later. Let's enjoy it now!&quot;<br /> <br /> That was some hardcore 18th century goth action.<br /> <br /> Bauhaus was 200 years late.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11558-nosimplehiway","name":"Nosimplehiway","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11558_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1699"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}